  "summary": "Tempus AI's $1.5 billion acquisition of Personalis marks a significant consolidation in AI-driven genomic testing, with the combined entity expecting substantial growth in cancer and other diagnostic tests. Separately, Bristol Myers Squibb joined other major pharma companies in announcing an NVIDIA-powered AI supercomputer for drug discovery, reflecting intensified competition to leverage advanced computing for pharmaceutical development. Several stories highlight emerging tensions in healthcare AI adoption: while providers are seeing EHR experience improvements and efficiency gains from AI rollouts, quality leaders warn that deploying AI at scale amplifies the risks of bad data, and healthcare systems need clear strategies for launching AI agents responsibly. On the regulatory and policy front, Medicare is proposing site-neutral payment reductions for certain hospital drugs and imaging services, while state audits and former employee accounts reveal that pharmacy benefit managers and telehealth companies like LifeMD are using opaque tactics that prioritize volume and profits over patient safety and cost transparency. Rural healthcare access remains a persistent challenge, and international efforts—including Singapore and New Zealand—are beginning to adapt foundation AI models for their own healthcare systems.",
